diff --git a/public/refund-policy.html b/public/refund-policy.html
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..69adcfb
--- /dev/null
+++ b/public/refund-policy.html
@@ -0,0 +1,144 @@
+<!doctype html>
+<html lang="en">
+<head>
+  <meta charset="utf-8">
+  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
+  <title>Refund Policy — EduQuinn</title>
+  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/styles.css">
+  <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.ico" sizes="any"><link rel="icon" href="/app-icon.svg" type="image/svg+xml"><link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/apple-touch-icon.png"><link rel="manifest" href="/manifest.webmanifest"><meta name="theme-color" content="#702632">
+</head>
+
+<body>
+
+<main class="policy-page">
+
+  <div class="policy-card">
+
+    <a href="/" class="policy-brand">
+      <span class="brand-mark">EQ</span>
+      <strong>EduQuinn</strong>
+    </a>
+
+    <h1>Refund Policy</h1>
+
+    <p class="policy-updated">
+      Last updated: November 2026
+    </p>
+
+    <p>
+      This policy explains when a course purchase on EduQuinn is eligible
+      for a refund, how to request one, and what happens to the instructor's
+      share of a sale that is refunded. It applies to the EduQuinn website at
+      eduquinn.co.zw and to the EduQuinn Android app. It works alongside our
+      <a href="/privacy-policy.html">Privacy Policy</a> and, for instructors,
+      the <a href="/instructor-policy.html">Instructor Terms &amp; Privacy
+      Policy</a>.
+    </p>
+
+    <h2>1. The short version</h2>
+    <p>
+      You can request a refund for a course within <strong>14 days</strong>
+      of purchase, as long as you have not completed more than
+      <strong>20%</strong> of that course yet. Requests inside this window
+      are processed automatically once submitted from your
+      <a href="/purchases.html">Purchase History</a> page. Outside this
+      window, or past that completion threshold, we may still consider a
+      refund at our discretion — contact <a href="/support.html">Support</a>
+      and explain the circumstances.
+    </p>
+
+    <h2>2. Why there's a completion limit as well as a time limit</h2>
+    <p>
+      EduQuinn is a marketplace: when you buy a course, the instructor who
+      made it is paid a share of what you paid (currently most of the
+      price). The 14-day/20% rule is there so that a genuine case —
+      bought the wrong course, it isn't what you expected, technical
+      problems prevented you from using it — can always get a refund
+      quickly, while discouraging someone from working through most of a
+      course and then asking for their money back once they've already
+      gotten the value out of it. If you believe your situation is a
+      genuine exception to the 20% limit, Support can still review it
+      manually.
+    </p>
+
+    <h2>3. How to request a refund</h2>
+    <ol>
+      <li>Open <a href="/purchases.html">Purchase History</a> while signed
+        in as a student.</li>
+      <li>Find the order and select <strong>Request refund</strong>. If the
+        order is still within the window above, this submits your request
+        immediately for review.</li>
+      <li>If the button isn't available, the order has either already
+        passed the 14-day window or more than 20% of a course in it has
+        been completed — contact <a href="/support.html">Support</a> if you
+        still believe a refund is warranted.</li>
+      <li>An administrator reviews every refund request before it is
+        approved or rejected. We aim to respond within a few business
+        days.</li>
+    </ol>
+
+    <h2>4. What happens once a refund is approved</h2>
+    <p>
+      Your access to the course is removed, and the payment is reversed
+      through the same method it was made with wherever our payment
+      processor, Paynow, supports that; where it doesn't, we arrange the
+      refund by bank transfer or mobile money instead. The instructor's
+      share of that sale is reversed along with it — refunded orders are
+      never paid out to an instructor. This is also why an instructor's
+      earnings on a new sale are held for a short period (currently a few
+      days longer than this policy's 14-day window) before being eligible
+      for payout: it gives any refund request time to be made and reviewed
+      first, rather than EduQuinn having to recover money from an
+      instructor's payout after the fact.
+    </p>
+
+    <h2>5. Free courses, coupons and bundles</h2>
+    <p>
+      A course with no charge (free, or fully covered by a coupon) has
+      nothing to refund. Where a coupon or bundle discount applied to a
+      paid order, an approved refund returns what was actually charged, not
+      the course's full list price.
+    </p>
+
+    <h2>6. Live classes and certificates</h2>
+    <p>
+      Reserving or attending a live class tied to a course does not by
+      itself affect refund eligibility, but it is taken into account as
+      part of how much of a course you've used. A certificate can only be
+      issued once a course reaches 100% completion, by which point an order
+      would already be well past the 20% threshold above and outside
+      automatic refund eligibility.
+    </p>
+
+    <h2>7. Chargebacks</h2>
+    <p>
+      If you dispute a charge directly with your bank or mobile money
+      provider instead of requesting a refund through EduQuinn first, your
+      course access may be suspended while the dispute is investigated.
+      Please contact <a href="/support.html">Support</a> first — it's
+      almost always faster.
+    </p>
+
+    <h2>8. Changes to this policy</h2>
+    <p>
+      We may update this policy, including the 14-day window and 20%
+      completion threshold, as the platform evolves. We will update the
+      "Last updated" date above when we do, and significant changes will be
+      announced on the platform.
+    </p>
+
+    <p class="policy-updated">
+      This policy is a plain-language summary provided for transparency and
+      app-store compliance purposes. It is not a substitute for legal
+      advice; EduQuinn's operator should have it reviewed by a qualified
+      lawyer familiar with Zimbabwe's consumer-protection and tax law before
+      relying on it, particularly regarding any withholding-tax treatment of
+      reversed instructor earnings.
+    </p>
+
+  </div>
+
+</main>
+
+</body>
+</html>
